stimate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEstimate a desk edge near 1 m using arm span or metre stick, then measure. Compare two ribbons by lining up ends or by both measures in cm.\n\nTRY FIRST\nEstimate then measure: your book length; a table width. Which estimate was closer?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswers vary; record estimate, measure and difference for each.","explain":"Estimating length uses benchmarks (for example a 30 cm ruler). Comparing lengths can use direct comparison or measured values in the same unit.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Comparing a centimetre measure with a metre measure without converting mis-orders lengths. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Estimate-then-measure path for length.","caption":"Use benchmarks like a metre stick.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Estimate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">about 1 m desk edge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">measure and compare</text></g